        "sourceB": "From the Black Panther Party Ten-Point Program, October 1966.\n\n\"We want freedom. We want power to determine the destiny of our Black Community. We want decent housing, fit for shelter of human beings. We want education for our people that exposes the true nature of this decadent American society.\"",
        "sourceC": "From an internal FBI memo written by Director J. Edgar Hoover to field offices, 1968.\n\n\"The Black Panther Party represents the single greatest threat to the internal security of the country. They are stockpiling weapons and preaching violent revolution. We must disrupt their leadership and destroy their community school breakfast programs, which they use to indoctrinate children.\"",
        "answer": "Source B is highly useful for showing the official socio-political platform and welfare goals of the Black Panthers. The content outlines their demands for political self-determination alongside basic social needs like \"decent housing\" and true history education. Its provenance as the official party manifesto means it provides direct, primary evidence of how the Panthers wanted to present their aims to the public.\n\nSource C is highly useful for revealing how the federal government viewed and targeted the Panthers. J. Edgar Hoover's description of them as a \"threat to security\" explains why the FBI launched its COINTELPRO campaign to destroy the party. However, it is highly biased, framing community social services like \"breakfast programs\" as dangerous brainwashing, which limits its objective accuracy about Panther motives while maximizing its utility for showing state hostility."
